// The privacy notice, at /privacy/: who is responsible, what the site
// collects (nothing), what GitHub logs as its host and under which
// safeguard it goes to the United States, what the browser keeps and how to
// clear it, and the visitor's rights, each statement citing the source it
// was read in. It is written to the Swiss Federal Act on Data Protection's
// duty to inform (Art. 19) and the GDPR's (Art. 13), and says that it is
// information, not legal advice. A page of its own, outside the prover, so
// that it is reached and read without the module; its links are absolute.
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

/** The sources, in the order the page cites them: [n] is SOURCES[n - 1]. */
const SOURCES = [
  {
    title:
      "Federal Act on Data Protection (FADP), SR 235.1, status of 1 September 2023; the English text is a translation without legal force",
    href: "https://www.fedlex.admin.ch/eli/cc/2022/491/en",
  },
  {
    title: "Data Protection Ordinance (DPO), SR 235.11, Annex 1, status of 15 September 2024",
    href: "https://www.fedlex.admin.ch/eli/cc/2022/568/en",
  },
  {
    title: "Telecommunications Act (TCA), SR 784.10, Art. 45c",
    href: "https://www.fedlex.admin.ch/eli/cc/1997/2187_2187_2187/en",
  },
  {
    title:
      "Federal Data Protection and Information Commissioner (FDPIC), New Swiss-US Data Privacy Framework, 15 August 2024",
    href: "https://www.edoeb.admin.ch/en/15082024-new-swiss-us-data-privacy-framework",
  },
  {
    title: "FDPIC, Submitting a complaint",
    href: "https://www.edoeb.admin.ch/en/submitting-a-complaint",
  },
  {
    title: "General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR), Regulation (EU) 2016/679",
    href: "https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32016R0679",
  },
  {
    title:
      "Commission Implementing Decision (EU) 2023/1795 of 10 July 2023 on the EU-US Data Privacy Framework",
    href: "https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=CELEX:32023D1795",
  },
  {
    title: "GitHub Docs, About GitHub Pages: Data collection",
    href: "https://docs.github.com/en/pages/getting-started-with-github-pages/about-github-pages#data-collection",
  },
  {
    title: "GitHub Privacy Statement, effective 27 April 2026",
    href: "https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement",
  },
  {
    title: "Data Privacy Framework List, the entry of GitHub, read on 23 October 2026",
    href: "https://www.dataprivacyframework.gov/participant/6174",
  },
] as const;
---

<Base
  title="Privacy: linlog"
  description="What linlog's site collects (nothing), what GitHub logs as its host, and what your browser keeps."
>
  <main class="page privacy-page">
    <p class="page-home"><a href="/">linlog</a></p>
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      linlog's prover runs in your browser. The site sets no cookies, tracks nothing and
      sends nothing you type anywhere. GitHub, which hosts it, logs your IP address.
    </p>

    <dl class="ledger" aria-label="Who has which of your data">
      <div>
        <dt>This site</dt>
        <dd>Nothing. No cookies, no analytics, nothing loaded from another server.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>GitHub, the host</dt>
        <dd>Your IP address, logged for security, in the United States among other places.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Your browser</dt>
        <dd>Your settings and open proofs, and the site's files for offline use. Never sent.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>

    <h2>Who is responsible</h2>
    <p>
      Fabian Lukas Grubmüller runs this site. Write to{" "}
      <a href="mailto:privacy@grubmueller.dev">privacy@grubmueller.dev</a> about anything on
      this page. The Swiss Federal Act on Data Protection asks for the controller's identity and
      contact details, the purpose of processing and its recipients, and, where data go
      abroad, the state and its safeguard <a href="#source-1" class="cite">[1, Art. 19]</a>; the
      GDPR, where it applies, asks for the same and the legal basis, how long data are kept
      and your rights <a href="#source-6" class="cite">[6, Art. 13]</a>. This page gives them.
    </p>

    <h2>What the site does not do</h2>
    <p>
      It sets no cookies and runs no analytics or tracking. Every file it loads comes from its
      own address: its Content-Security-Policy, in the head of every page, starts with{" "}
      <code>default-src 'none'</code> and allows only the site itself. The prover is linlog's
      library compiled to WebAssembly, running in your browser: the sequents you type and the
      proofs you make are computed there and sent nowhere.
    </p>

    <h2>What GitHub logs</h2>
    <p>
      The site is served by GitHub Pages. GitHub says: “When a GitHub Pages site is visited,
      the visitor's IP address is logged and stored for security purposes, regardless of
      whether the visitor has signed into GitHub or not” <a href="#source-8" class="cite">[8]</a>.
      For its practices GitHub points to its privacy statement, which names the United States
      among the places where GitHub stores and processes personal data, and gives its privacy
      contact <a href="#source-9" class="cite">[9]</a>.
      Your browser has to send your IP address to fetch any page, so the site cannot be loaded
      without it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Where the GDPR applies, this rests on a legitimate interest{" "}
      <a href="#source-6" class="cite">[6, Art. 6(1)(f)]</a>: a site cannot be served without
      its visitor's address, and its host keeps the address to keep the service secure. For how
      long, GitHub's statement says it retains personal data as needed to fulfil contractual
      obligations, comply with legal requirements, resolve disputes and enforce agreements{" "}
      <a href="#source-9" class="cite">[9]</a>.
    </p>

    <h3>The safeguard for the United States</h3>
    <p>
      Swiss law lets personal data go to a state the Federal Council has found adequate{" "}
      <a href="#source-1" class="cite">[1, Art. 16(1)]</a>. For the United States, its list
      grants this for data processed by organisations certified under the Swiss-US Data
      Privacy Framework <a href="#source-2" class="cite">[2, Annex 1]</a>, from 15 September
      2024 <a href="#source-4" class="cite">[4]</a>. The European Commission found the same for
      organisations on the framework's list, under the EU-US framework{" "}
      <a href="#source-7" class="cite">[7, Art. 1]</a>; the GDPR allows a transfer under such a
      decision <a href="#source-6" class="cite">[6, Art. 45(1)]</a>. GitHub states that it
      complies with the EU-US framework, its UK extension and the Swiss-US framework{" "}
      <a href="#source-9" class="cite">[9]</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      GitHub's entry on the framework's list, read on 23 October 2026, shows the organisation
      “GitHub”, of San Francisco, with all three certifications active (EU-US, its UK
      extension, and Swiss-US), each covering data other than human-resources data and running
      to 3 August 2027 <a href="#source-10" class="cite">[10]</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>What your browser keeps</h2>
    <p>
      To remember your work, the page writes to your browser's own storage. Nothing of it
      leaves the browser, and none of it identifies you.
    </p>
    <table class="kept">
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Kept as</th><th scope="col">What, and why</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>linlog-settings</code></td>
          <td>
            Your settings (the search's budget, the net's drawing, the fonts, keys shown), so
            they hold on your next visit. A forced engine is not kept.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>linlog-theme</code></td>
          <td>Light or dark, when you pinned one, so the page opens in it.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>linlog-proofs:…</code>, <code>linlog-proofs-previous</code></td>
          <td>
            Your open proofs, saved as you work, one set per browser tab, so the next visit can
            bring them back.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>The service worker's cache, <code>linlog-site-…</code></td>
          <td>
            The site's own files, so the prover works without a network after one visit; a
            math font you choose is added the first time you use it.
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Swiss law allows data to be kept on your device when you are told what is kept and why,
      and that you may refuse <a href="#source-3" class="cite">[3, Art. 45c]</a>. You may: clear
      the site's data in your browser's settings for <code>linlog-prover.github.io</code>,
      which removes all of the above, the service worker included; or block site data, or use
      a private window. The prover works the same, only it remembers nothing between visits.
    </p>

    <h2>Your rights</h2>
    <p>
      Under the Swiss Act you may ask whether data about you are processed and receive them,
      free of charge, as a rule within 30 days <a href="#source-1" class="cite">[1, Art. 25]</a>;
      have incorrect data corrected, and in an action before a court ask that a processing or
      a disclosure be prohibited or that data be deleted{" "}
      <a href="#source-1" class="cite">[1, Art. 32]</a>. You may report a processing you think
      breaks the Act to the Federal Data Protection and Information Commissioner, who opens an
      investigation where there are sufficient indications{" "}
      <a href="#source-1" class="cite">[1, Art. 49]</a>; the Commissioner asks that you write to
      the controller first <a href="#source-5" class="cite">[5]</a>. Where the GDPR applies, you
      have the rights of access, rectification, erasure, restriction, portability and objection{" "}
      <a href="#source-6" class="cite">[6, Arts. 15 to 18, 20, 21]</a>, and may lodge a complaint
      with a supervisory authority, in particular where you live or work{" "}
      <a href="#source-6" class="cite">[6, Art. 77]</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      This site holds no data about you, so a request to me will usually find none. For
      GitHub's logs, write to GitHub through the contact its privacy statement gives{" "}
      <a href="#source-9" class="cite">[9]</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>When you write</h2>
    <p>
      An e-mail to the address above is read and kept only to answer it. An issue on GitHub
      is GitHub's service, under its privacy statement <a href="#source-9" class="cite">[9]</a>.
      A link to another site (the licences, the papers cited in Learn) leaves this one; that
      site's own rules apply there.
    </p>

    <p class="sub">
      This page says how the site handles data, checked against the sources below on 23 October
      2026. It is information, not legal advice. Where a statement here goes beyond what its
      source says word for word, it is this page's reading of that source.
    </p>

    <h2 id="sources">Sources</h2>
    <ol class="sources">
      {
        SOURCES.map((source, i) => (
          <li id={`source-${i + 1}`}>
            <a href={source.href}>{source.title}</a>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ol>
  </main>
</Base>
